<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nazi Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as NSDAP, National Socialist German Workers' Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nazi Party, the National Socialist German Workers' Party, was the far-right party led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> from 1921. Founded in 1920 from the German Workers' Party, it rose during the Depression to become the largest party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> in 1932, and after Hitler became chancellor in 1933 it was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s only legal party. It was banned after Germany's defeat in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nazi-party.jpg" alt="Nazi Party" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RsVe, corrected by Barliner. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parteiadler_Nationalsozialistische_Deutsche_Arbeiterpartei_(1933%E2%80%931945).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party grew out of small nationalist groups formed in the last years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, in the culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> paramilitaries that fought communist uprisings in postwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On 7 March 1918 Anton Drexler, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> locksmith, formed a branch of a league called the Free Workers' Committee for a good Peace. He opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, held antisemitic, anti-monarchist and anti-Marxist views, and called for a union of völkisch nationalism with economic socialism that could draw workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. The renaming of the German Workers' Party added Socialist and Workers' to appeal to the left, and National and German to the right.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hitler was pardoned and released from prison on 20 December 1924. The ban on the party was lifted and it was formally refounded on 26 February 1925, with Hitler as undisputed leader. It disavowed any intention of taking power by force and followed what he called the path of legality. After Hitler became chancellor on 30 January 1933, the Nazis carried out Gleichschaltung, the coordination of German government and society. In June and July 1933 competing parties were outlawed or dissolved themselves, and the Law Against the Formation of Parties of 14 July 1933 made the Nazi Party's monopoly legal.</p><p>Central to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> was the idea of a people's community, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksgemeinschaft">Volksgemeinschaft</a>, uniting Germans deemed racially desirable and excluding political dissidents and people deemed inferior or foreign. The party sought to disenfranchise, segregate and eventually exterminate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, disabled people, homosexuals, Jehovah's Witnesses and political opponents. The Final Solution, a system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, killed around 6 million Jews and millions of other victims in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Instrument of Surrender was signed on 8 May 1945, and the Allied Control Council formally abolished the party on 10 October 1945. Denazification followed alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a>, where several top leaders were found guilty of crimes against humanity. The use of its symbols remains outlawed in many European countries, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nazi Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prussia banned the <b>Nazi Party</b> in 1922, the Red Front Fighters after Blood May in 1929 and the SA in 1931.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>National Socialist German Workers’ Party</b> (NSDAP), led by Adolf Hitler since 1921, had failed in the Beer Hall Putsch of 1923 but became the largest party in the Reichstag in July 1932, winning 37 per cent of the vote amid the mass unemployment of the Great Depression.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Unemployment reached 4 million in 1930, and in the election of September 1930 the <b>Nazi Party</b> took 18.3% of the vote.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1931 the <b>Nazi Party</b> had a majority on the Dessau city council, and the next year it moved to close the school.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In Germany Hitler’s government banned or forced the dissolution of every other party, and on 14 July 1933 made the <b>Nazi Party</b> the only legal one.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Adolf Hitler began to model himself and the <b>Nazi Party</b> on Mussolini within a month, and in November 1923 the Nazis attempted a “March on Berlin” that ended in the failed Beer Hall Putsch in Munich.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
